  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{2}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({2}^{1}-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{2}^{x}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{2}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037234/assets/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}\\cdot (2-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
